<column class="doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">Introduction</p>
  <h1>Getting started</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A SUI component is a <code>.sui</code> file — markup, styling as
    <code>sx</code> token utilities, and optional state in an inline
    <code>&lt;controller&gt;</code> block. The compiler turns it into a <strong>standard
    custom element</strong> — a real web component you can use in any page, any
    framework, or none. No virtual DOM, no framework runtime wrapped around it.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <p>SUI is a set of packages. Use whatever package manager you like.</p>
  <code-block code={code.install}></code-block>

  <h2>Write a component</h2>
  <p>
    A <code>&lt;component&gt;</code> block declaring what comes in, and markup
    that reads it. Text takes the type scale automatically; reach for
    <code>sx</code> when you need surface or spacing. No controller needed until
    there's state to own.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.component}></code-block>

  <h2>Compile it</h2>
  <p>
    The <code>sui-compiler</code> lowers the <code>.sui</code> into a small JS
    module that defines the custom element. That module is the whole output —
    it imports only <code>@ship/sui-runtime</code>.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.compile}></code-block>

  <h2>Use it anywhere</h2>
  <p>
    Importing the compiled module registers the element with the browser. From
    then on <code>&lt;sui-greeting&gt;</code> is a first-class HTML element —
    drop it into a static page, a React app, a Svelte app, or plain HTML. It's
    a web component; nothing about SUI has to be present in the host.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.use}></code-block>

  <h2>Add state</h2>
  <p>
    When a component needs state, add a <code>&lt;controller&gt;</code> block to
    the same file. The markup reads what the controller's <code>scope()</code>
    returns — one file, logic and template together.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.interactive}></code-block>

  <h2>The single-file model</h2>
  <p>One file, two roles — the separation is the whole mental model:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The template</strong> (the markup outside the block) is bindings —
      a pure read of state. It reads signals (<code>&#123;count()&#125;</code>),
      wires events (<code>on:click</code>), and binds inputs
      (<code>bind:value</code>). It holds no logic.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The <code>&lt;controller&gt;</code> block</strong> owns the state
      and the logic. Its <code>scope()</code> returns the signals, derived
      values, and handlers the template binds to; <code>mount(state, host)</code>
      runs side effects once the element is in the DOM.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The template can only bind names the controller's <code>scope()</code> hands
    it — the compiler enforces it. Markup on one side, state on the other,
    nothing tangled between them.
  </p>

  <h2>deck — optional</h2>
  <p>
    Everything above is standalone: the compiler produces web components, and
    that's the whole story. For a full <em>application</em> — bundling, a
    hot-reloading dev loop, routing, server rendering — <a href="/deck">deck</a>
    is the optional app framework that drives the compiler for you. SUI works
    with it or without it.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.deck}></code-block>

  <row class="page-nav">
    <a class="btn" href="/">← Overview</a>
    <a class="btn" href="/reactivity">Reactivity →</a>
  </row>
</column>
